               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="110X/111X" notBefore="1100" notAfter="1119"
                        calendar="#gregorian">s. xii in.</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#e s" source="6742"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_469">Rochester,
                        Kent. Cathedral priory of St Andrew</orgName><p>Erasure at foot of fol. 1
                        where the Rochester inscription should be; it was no doubt (letters in
                        italics are legible under UV): <q>Li<hi rend="italic">b</hi>er <hi
                              rend="italic">de</hi> c<hi rend="italic">l</hi>aust<hi rend="italic"
                              >r</hi>o Roffense <hi rend="italic">per</hi> J . . . . <hi
                              rend="italic">monachum</hi></q>. Medieval contents list on fol. ii (10
                        items): identifiable with an entry in the medieval Rochester catalogue in
                           <title>Textus Roffensis</title> (<title>Archaeologia Cantiana</title> VI,
                        p. 124) [cat. of 1202 no. 45]. The script is partly of a Rochester type and
                        the binding is similar to another Rochester manuscript, Bodley 134, and like
                        that has the words 'S Wigorniae' on one of the endleaves (s.
                     xvi).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance source="5856"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_596">Windsor, Berkshire.
                        Royal collegiate chapel of St George</orgName><p>Medieval binding resembles
                        Bodl. 134. Usual chain mark.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_86">Bury St Edmunds,
                        Suffolk, Benedictine abbey of St Edmund, King and Martyr</orgName>
                     <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of
                        Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The
                        corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card originally
                        recorded the following information: <q>M. R. James, <title>Catalogue of
                              Pembroke College MSS</title>, pp. ix, xxxvii. Perhaps Bury, then
                           Pembroke College Cambridge no. 194 = 2122</q>. Neil Ker later added a
                        summative note to the card: <q>Bogus: it is from Rochester (N. R.
                        K.)</q></p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_4">Canterbury, Kent,
                        Benedictine cathedral priory of Holy Trinity or Christ Church</orgName>
                     <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition (1964), which did not
                        change in the Supplement (1987). The corresponding index card is mostly
                        blank.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_601">Worcester,
                        Worcestershire, Benedictine cathedral priory of St Mary the Virgin</orgName>
                     <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition (1964), which did not
                        change in the Supplement (1987). The corresponding index card records the
                        following information, which was deemed unsatisfactory for an attribution:
                              <q><q>Wigornie</q> (fol. 246v). For possible connection with Worcester
                           see <title>Summary Catalogue</title> &amp; C. H. Turner, <title>Early
                              Worcester Manuscripts</title>, p. lxvi.</q></p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Dean and Canons of Windsor,
                     1612.</provenance>
               </history>
